# 36. Installing packages

`hql package` builds a package from its crate and keeps it under the HQL
home, where every program on the machine reaches it. This chapter is the
commands, the files they write, and the two settings of `hql.toml` that
concern packages.

## The commands

| Command | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `hql package install --path <dir>` | build the package of a crate on this machine and install it |
| `hql package list` | the installed packages, with the modules each provides |
| `hql package info <name>` | what an installed package records about itself |
| `hql package rebuild <name>` | build an installed package again from its source |
| `hql package remove <name>` | remove an installed package |

Installing needs `cargo` on the `PATH`
([rustup](https://rustup.rs) installs it). Listing, describing and
removing need nothing but `hql`.

## Installing

```sh
git clone https://github.com/ewiger/hql && cd hql
hql package install --path tests/packages/hello
```

```text
   Compiling hql-ext-hello v0.1.0
    Finished `release` profile [optimized] target(s) in 58.20s
installed hello 0.1.0 (hello) in /home/ada/.hql/lib/hello
```

The first build of a package compiles the `hql` crate and its
dependencies, which takes about a minute; later builds reuse them. The
last line is written once the package has been built, installed and
loaded: a package that builds and cannot be loaded is reported here, with
the reason, and not left for a program to find.

```sh
hql package list
```

```text
hello 0.1.0 (hello)
```

```sh
hql eval 'import hello
hello.greet("Ada")'
```

```text
"Hello, Ada!"
```

## What is installed

A package is a directory under the HQL home, `~/.hql` unless `$HQL_HOME`
names another:

```text
~/.hql/lib/hello/
├── package.toml
├── modules/
│   └── hello/mod.hql
└── libhql_ext_hello.dylib
```

`package.toml` is the record of the build:

```toml
[package]
name = "hello"
crate = "hql-ext-hello"
version = "0.1.0"
provides = ["hello"]

[source]
kind = "path"
path = "/home/ada/hql/tests/packages/hello"

[build]
hql = "0.7.0"
rustc = "1.98.1 (48a229cea 2026-09-01)"
target = "aarch64-apple-darwin"
library = "libhql_ext_hello.dylib"
built = "2026-11-14T10:02:11Z"
```

| Table | Records |
| --- | --- |
| `[package]` | the package, its crate and version, and the modules it provides |
| `[source]` | where the source came from, which is what `rebuild` builds again |
| `[build]` | the `hql` and the compiler it was built with, and the library's file name |

The directory is self-contained. Removing it removes the package, which
is all `hql package remove` does. The crate it was built from is not
needed again until a rebuild.

## Describing and rebuilding

```sh
hql package info hello
```

```text
package: hello
crate: hql-ext-hello 0.1.0
provides: hello
source: path /home/ada/hql/tests/packages/hello
built: 2026-11-14T10:02:11Z against hql 0.7.0 with rustc 1.98.1 (48a229cea 2026-09-01) for aarch64-apple-darwin
library: /home/ada/.hql/lib/hello/libhql_ext_hello.dylib
```

After `hql` is upgraded, an installed package was built for the previous
one and is refused until it is rebuilt
([[35-how-a-library-is-loaded/the-version-rule|the version rule]]).
`hql package list` says which:

```text
hello 0.1.0 (hello): built against hql 0.7.0, run `hql package rebuild hello`
```

```sh
hql package rebuild hello
```

## A package under development

Installing copies a build. While a package is being written, a workspace
names the crate instead, under `[packages] paths` in its `hql.toml`:

```toml
[packages]
paths = ["../hello"]
```

A path is read against the directory of the `hql.toml`. Each crate listed
is built the first time a program runs in the workspace, and again
whenever its `Cargo.toml`, a file under `src/` or a file under its module
root is newer than the build. The build is kept under the crate's own
`target/` directory, and nothing is written to the HQL home.

<!-- run: --workspace examples/packages -->
```hql
import hello

Greeting("Ada").length
```

```text
11
```

The workspace of that program is
[`examples/packages/`](https://github.com/ewiger/hql/tree/main/examples/packages),
whose `hql.toml` lists the `hello` crate. A crate a workspace lists comes
before an installed package of the same name, so the checkout being edited
is the one that runs.

## Importing a package for every program

A module of a package is imported where it is used, as any module outside
the prelude is. A workspace whose programs all use one may import it once,
under `[extensions] import` in `hql.toml`, by the package's name:

```toml
[extensions]
import = ["hello"]
```

Every program of the workspace then starts with the package's modules
imported, and `greet("Ada")` needs no `import` line. The setting names a
standard module or a package; it is the one place a package is named.

## Where a module comes from

| Source | Found in | Wins over |
| --- | --- | --- |
| a builtin package | the `hql` binary | everything: no package provides a module `std` or `search` does |
| a crate under `[packages] paths` | the workspace's `hql.toml` | an installed package of the same name |
| an installed package | `~/.hql/lib/` | a workspace module of the same name |
| a workspace module | the module roots ([[05-modules|chapter 5]]) | |

`hql builtins` prints the module graph a program in the current directory
would see, each module with the package and version providing it.

